BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 487.62 Tm (mailboxes an absolute game changer and names a custom SMTP server the worst possible setup for) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 472.40 Tm (landing in the inbox [8EaKo_iAiPE @ 00:02:42]. On density, the numbers converge: two to three mailboxes) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 457.17 Tm (per domain from Kyle Dickson, who warns that if the domain gets destroyed every inbox on it dies with it) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 441.95 Tm ([drsocawArfo @ 00:04:41]. Coldbot frames the domain as the building and mailboxes as rooms, so one) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 426.72 Tm (hundred addresses on one new domain is still concentrated risk, and argues that Google Workspace's) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 411.50 Tm (technical ceiling is not a cold-outreach target; Instantly's documentation recommends around thirty) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 396.27 Tm (campaign emails per day for regular mailboxes [fM-ywBq8yoU @ 00:03:43]. The working rule that falls out:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 381.05 Tm (when more volume is needed, add healthy inboxes and domains rather than turning one inbox into a fire) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 365.82 Tm (hose.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 350.60 Tm (Authentication and warmup are the table stakes nobody skips, and nobody oversells. Smartlead's team,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 335.37 Tm (claiming 2 million cold emails sent in a year and more than 300 campaigns reviewed, lists four) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 320.15 Tm (non-negotiables before warmup even begins, with SPF, DKIM, DMARC, and MX records all set up and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 304.92 Tm (verified at the top of the list [DIUObYQih3s @ 00:02:27]. TechSales compresses the trio into three questions) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 289.70 Tm (an operator can remember: SPF answers who can send, DKIM answers whether the message is really from) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 274.47 Tm (you, and DMARC decides what to do if it does not check out [aa7B2gisZdU @ 00:05:26]. Kyle Dickson's) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 259.25 Tm (version is the bluntest of all: warm every inbox two to three weeks minimum before a single cold send, keep) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 244.02 Tm (warmup running forever, and never shut it off [drsocawArfo @ 00:00:15]. Coldbot supplies the necessary) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 228.80 Tm (cold water: you can have SPF, DKIM, and DMARC and still get poor placement, because authentication) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 213.57 Tm (proves identity but does not force Gmail or Outlook to like your sending behavior, and a warmed inbox can) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 198.35 Tm (still be damaged by bad data, complaints, or sudden volume spikes [fM-ywBq8yoU @ 00:05:42]. The) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 183.12 Tm (consensus position is that both are prerequisites, not guarantees, and the infrastructure has to be monitored) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 167.90 Tm (after launch rather than treated as a one-time checklist.) Tj ET
BT /F0 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 145.67 Tm (Infrastructure rules multiple practitioners agree on before the first cold send) Tj ET
